Fixed data model, Elasticsearch and device module bugs found while expanding the data model (`#48 <https://github.com/sandialabs/PEAT/issues/48>`_):

- Textual event severities (e.g. ``warning``) were sent as ``event.severity``/``host.event.severity``, which are numbers in Elasticsearch, and caused mapping errors. Numeric severities (within the Elasticsearch ``long`` range) are now sent as numbers, and textual or out-of-range ones as ``log.level``.
- ``original`` file contents inside the ``files`` and ``module`` lists were exported in host documents and ``device-data-*.json`` files.
- ``host.files`` and ``host.successful_pulls`` had no explicit Elasticsearch mapping, and the ``hash.*`` hashes of an event's ``original`` text had no mapping in the events index.
- An empty (disabled) log, events, memory or files index name stopped the saving of all Elasticsearch documents to ``ELASTIC_DIR``.
- De-duplication of nested lists (e.g. the services of an interface) after merging two devices was discarded.
- ``Interface.description`` (an object) was converted to a string by a validator.
- Sorting lists with both timezone-naive and timezone-aware times (e.g. events) crashed.
- ``peat parse`` did not push devices created during the parse (e.g. the other devices of a Sage or ION multi-device parse) to Elasticsearch.
- SEL: ETH overflow counters (``$$$$``) were reported as ``-1``; ``ESNTP=UNICAST`` was reported as disabled; ``DNPUDP=REQ`` crashed port settings parsing; ``get_historical_events`` could raise ``StopIteration``; the RTAC web password was read from the cached user name, a ``del`` was a no-op, the MainController tasks were assigned twice, a ``regsiter_stop`` typo, and an invalid ECS event category on the RTAC monitor alert; RTAC RS-232 ports had the type ``rs-232`` instead of ``rs_232``; the SEL-451 UTC offset was formatted as ``-08:0``; the FTP trace log contained passwords; zlib-compressed ``.CID`` files were decompressed without a size limit; files pulled via Telnet, serial or older FTP lost their directory in ``files`` paths and ``logic.file.path`` (e.g. ``/SET_ALL.TXT`` instead of ``/SETTINGS/SET_ALL.TXT``); an ``EDNP`` DNP3 session count above 1 was reported as disabled.
- SIPROTEC: the uptime was 100 times too large (SNMP TimeTicks are hundredths of a second); the bad-community counters were read from the wrong OIDs; ``ifOperStatus`` values were mapped incorrectly; the system name was cut off when it had no space.
- M340: a ``deepcopy`` crash and unreachable service code; ``module_<slot>`` data was overwritten; a short LED row dropped all SNMP data; ``ifTableLastChange`` was reported as the uptime; the firmware version was duplicated; parsing SNMP and NTP settings from the project file crashed on bytes.
- Sage: every route was a copy of the last route; memory conversion stopped at the first bad task; the USERLOG dataset and action were never set; ``ethernet.xml`` entries were overwritten; ``ACCESS.XML`` passwords were copied into ``dev.extra``.
- GE UR relays: pages were downloaded twice; the type of ``lo0`` was not ``loopback``.
- FortiGate: SNMP manager hosts were never captured; NIC counters, speed and link state were lost with the documented ``Rx_Packets=`` output format of ``diagnose hardware deviceinfo nic``; events without a ``level`` crashed parsing; an unknown NIC ``State``/``Link`` value aborted the debug log parse.
- ControlLogix: ``ifLastChange`` was used as the interface uptime and crashed when missing; FTP pulls tried to download directories. L5X: the IP addresses of remote modules were copied onto the controller.
- iDirect used the Sage options (``options["sage"]``); Linux and BusyBox ``ifconfig`` output crashed the ``ifconfig`` parser.
- Camlin Totus: the Wi-Fi hotspot password was stored in ``dev.extra`` and the JSON side files. It and other password, PSK and secret values are now redacted.
- easyGen serial pulls never ran (interface type mismatch); OpenPLC task timing statistics in the new runtime format were not parsed; Windows CE ``HKEY_USERS`` was never saved (``HKEY_CLASSES_ROOT`` was copied twice); MicroNet file paths (doubled file names, wrong local paths) and a crash when the logs had no GAP event.
